            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <p>Account suspension is one of the most frustrating issues in overseas advertising. Many teams file appeals immediately without results, getting rejected two or three times in a row. The real problem is not the number of appeals submitted, but the lack of clarity on the actual violation reason.</p>
<h2>Key Takeaway: Success Depends on Precision, Not Volume</h2>
<p>A winning appeal doesn't require mountains of documentation. It requires three things: <strong>accurate violation identification, documented corrective actions, and credible prevention mechanisms</strong>. Many advertisers treat appeals as appeals for leniency, but Google's policy compliance team follows a process. What they need to see is your concrete ability to solve the problem.</p>
<h2>Step One: Identify the Violation Type, Don't Guess</h2>
<p>Before preparing any appeal materials, the first step is to check the suspension notice in your Google Ads account. Common violation categories include:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Circumventing systems</strong>: Using domain changes or modified landing pages to bypass review</li>
<li><strong>Misleading content</strong>: Exaggerating product benefits or fabricating user testimonials</li>
<li><strong>Brand or trademark infringement</strong>: Using unauthorized third-party brand elements in creatives</li>
<li><strong>Malware or unsafe redirects</strong>: Landing pages containing viruses or phishing risks</li>
<li><strong>Invalid landing pages or irrelevant content</strong>: URLs unreachable or page content mismatching ad promises</li>
</ul>
<p>Different violation types require vastly different appeal materials. Brand infringement needs trademark authorization documentation, while landing page issues require screenshots of corrected URLs with timestamps. Submitting generic appeal templates not only wastes effort but may be flagged as "repeated invalid submissions," slowing future reviews.</p>
<h2>Step Two: Evidence Must Be Specific, With a Clear Timeline</h2>
<p>The most persuasive part of an appeal isn't written explanations—it's <strong>verifiable evidence and a clear remediation timeline</strong>. For landing page issues specifically:</p>
<p><strong>Effective evidence formats</strong>:<br />
- Screenshots of corrected pages (with URL and date watermarks)<br />
- Server access logs proving the page is restored<br />
- Compliance reports from third-party tools like Google PageSpeed Insights</p>
<p><strong>Common ineffective evidence</strong>:<br />
- Subjective statements like "We've fixed it, please trust us"<br />
- Screenshots without timestamps or URLs, making verification impossible<br />
- Submitting old page screenshots claiming they're already corrected</p>
<p>A utility app team once had their account suspended due to an unreachable landing page. The advertiser submitted screenshots of the corrected page, but the review team found the URL in the screenshots didn't match the URL registered in the account, leading to rejection. <strong>Evidence must form a closed loop with account backend information</strong>—this is the detail most easily overlooked.</p>
<h2>Step Three: Prevention Mechanisms Must Be Specific, Not Vague</h2>
<p>After appeal approval, accounts typically enter a probation period where any policy violation leads to permanent suspension. Therefore, appeals must proactively explain prevention mechanisms rather than waiting to be asked.</p>
<p>Prevention mechanisms should cover three dimensions:</p>
<p><strong>Personnel</strong>: Who is responsible for creative review, what is the review process, and how are approval permissions structured?<br />
<strong>Technology</strong>: Are automated tools being used to detect keyword and landing page compliance?<br />
<strong>Process</strong>: Has the pre-launch creative compliance checklist been updated?</p>
<p>Avoid vague promises like "We'll be more careful next time." Policy reviewers have seen countless templated commitments. Only specific operational procedures demonstrate genuine remediation intent.</p>
<h2>Conclusion: Appeals Are Problem-Solving Demonstrations, Not Petitions</h2>
<p>Google Ads account appeals are essentially demonstrations to reviewers that you've identified the problem, understood the policy, completed remediation, and established prevention mechanisms. Rather than spending energy on emotional petitions, focus on verifying violation reasons, collecting verifiable evidence, and documenting remediation procedures.</p>
<p>If your account is suspended for policy violations, stop and ask yourself three questions: Have I confirmed the violation reason? Do I have evidence of remediation? Have I specified prevention mechanisms? Answering these three questions clearly will significantly improve your appeal success rate.</p>
